API layer
=========

Everything that crosses the network or touches AsyncStorage lives in
src/services/. Screens never call fetch and never call AsyncStorage.


Files
-----

	apiClient.js          the fetch wrapper, base URL, auth header, refresh
	tokenStore.js         access and refresh tokens in AsyncStorage
	authApi.js            register, login, verify, forgot, reset, refresh
	userApi.js            the live profile
	challengeApi.js       challenges, join, submit result
	questApi.js           the quest bank and the daily rotation
	catalogApi.js         facility areas and sub-activities
	customActivityApi.js  per-user custom activities and pinning
	checkinApi.js         gym check-in and its status
	statsApi.js           summary, exercise list, per-exercise series
	leaderboardApi.js     faculty and campus boards
	badgeApi.js           badge catalogue and earned badges
	pushTokenStore.js     the registered Expo push token
	pinStore.js           pinned exercises for the home widget
	settingsStore.js      quest reminders and auto check-in toggles
	realtime.js           the Socket.IO connection and the event bridge


apiClient
---------

	export const API_BASE_URL =
	  process.env.EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL || "http://localhost:8000/api";

The value must end in /api and must be an address the device can reach. On a
phone, localhost is the phone. Use the machine's LAN address or an Expo
tunnel.

api(path, { method, body, auth }) does the whole request:

* attaches Authorization: Bearer <access token> unless auth is false,
* JSON encodes the body and parses the response defensively, so an HTML error
  page from a proxy becomes a message rather than a parse crash,
* on a 401, attempts exactly one refresh and replays the request once. If the
  refresh fails it clears the stored auth, which sends AppShell back to the
  sign-in stack,
* throws an Error carrying status, message and any field errors array, so a
  screen can show validation feedback inline.

The single-retry guard is the _retried flag. Do not remove it; without it a
dead refresh token produces an infinite request loop.


Adding a call
-------------

1. Add a function to the right service file. Return the payload the screen
   needs, not the raw envelope.
2. If more than one screen needs the result, add an action to AppContext and
   call it from there. If one screen needs it, the screen may call the service
   directly.
3. Match the backend serializer field for field. If the serializer sends
   facultyKey and level, carry them through instead of flattening them; a
   board that hardcodes a faculty renders every student identically, which is
   a defect this project has already shipped once.


Realtime
--------

services/realtime.js owns the socket. It connects with the access token in the
handshake auth field and bridges server events to local behaviour:

	challenge:created       refetch challenges, raise a local banner
	validation:submitted    banner
	validation:decided      banner and refetch

Two things about this file are load bearing:

* Listeners are rebound once the socket actually exists. The socket is created
  after AppContext mounts, so a naive bind at mount time attaches to null and
  silently never fires. Any new listener must go through the hook in this
  file rather than reaching for the socket directly.
* Banners are gated on the user's settings. A quest reminder banner checks
  questReminders through settingsStore before showing.


Push notifications
------------------

	hooks/usePushNotifications.js   registers the device with Expo and posts
	                                the token to POST /push/register
	services/pushTokenStore.js      remembers the token so logout can
	                                DELETE /push/register

Registration only runs when authenticated, which is why PushGate sits inside
AuthProvider in App.js. The backend sends through the Expo push service.

Push needs a physical device. It cannot be tested in a simulator.


Local stores
------------

	tokenStore.js      getAccessToken, getRefreshToken, updateAccessToken,
	                   clearAuth
	pinStore.js        loadPinned, togglePinned, savePinned, clearPinned,
	                   ensurePinsInitialized
	settingsStore.js   loadSettings, getSetting, setSetting, subscribe

settingsStore caches in memory and notifies subscribers, so a toggle applies
without a reload. getSetting is synchronous against that cache, which is why
loadSettings must run at startup. See docs/maintenance.md.